# Line endings, normalised so a file is the same size on every machine.
#
# WHY THIS LANDED WITH THE TOOLING AND NOT WITH T1
# ------------------------------------------------
# `scripts/check-claude-size.mjs` enforces a character ceiling on CLAUDE.md. Without this file,
# `core.autocrlf=true` makes that measurement platform-dependent: the same CLAUDE.md is 51,761
# bytes in this Windows working tree and 51,306 in a Linux checkout — 455 bytes apart, purely in
# line endings, with not one character of content different.
#
# A ceiling that means something different per contributor is a value that moves embedded in a
# permanent check, which is the failure CLAUDE.md's own "Never embed a value that moves" rule
# names. The script defends itself by normalising CRLF before counting, but the honest fix is for
# the bytes on disk not to differ in the first place.
#
# It also retires the status-vs-diff quirk this repo has lived with: with autocrlf and no
# attributes file, writing LF into a tracked file leaves `git status` calling it modified while
# `git diff` shows nothing changed.
#
# SAFE TO ADD MID-HISTORY, VERIFIED
# ---------------------------------
# `git ls-files --eol` reports every tracked text file as `i/lf` — the index is already pure LF,
# because autocrlf has been converting on the way in all along. So `text=auto eol=lf` changes no
# blob content and needs no `--renormalize` commit; it only fixes what lands in the working tree
# on checkout. Adding this to a repo whose index held CRLF would rewrite every file at once, so
# check before copying this into another project.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the one text format that genuinely requires CRLF — cmd.exe will not reliably
# parse an LF-only .bat. There are none in the repo today; this is here so adding one later does
# not quietly break it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary. `text=auto` already detects all seven of these correctly today, but detection is a guess
# made from file content and it only has to be wrong once, on a PNG whose first bytes happen to
# look like text, to corrupt an image on checkout.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
